3. 4B's 5,120-BYTE EMBEDDING ROW — the options, costed
==============================================================================
    vocab 248,320; packed row 2*H = 5120 B; next power of two 8192 B

    (a) PAD to 8192 B      table 1940.0 MiB (+727.5 MiB, +60 %), EMBLOG2 = 13
        RTL delta NONE. Host delta: gen_model_script writes padded rows; manifest emb_row_bytes = 8192.
        Channel-0 budget: W_BASE 256 + weights + table 1940.0 MiB
          all:w8g128  -> 3225.6 MiB of 4096 (78.7 %)  FITS
          all:w4g128  -> 2722.6 MiB of 4096 (66.5 %)  FITS

    (b) NON-POWER-OF-TWO row addressing in RTL
        5120 = 4096 + 1024 = (x<<12) + (x<<10): a TWO-TERM shift-add,
        not a general multiplier.  rtl/seq_unit.sv:842-843 today is
        one shift and one add; this makes it two shifts and two adds.
        Saves 727.5 MiB of DDR that the channel demonstrably has (see (a)).
        COST: it lands in the SEQ MOV/EMB address path, which is one
        of the three classes that carried waived endpoints on
        build_034 (evidence/qwen2b/rb/TIMING.md: seq_0 MOV addressing,
        3 endpoints).  build_035 closes at WNS 0.000 with ZERO margin
        (RD_GATE 6), so any added logic there re-opens timing.
        Also needs the EMBLOG2 CSR to become a row-BYTES field or a
        2-term encoding; today it is a 4-bit log2 with range [8,13].

    (c) NARROW the embedding to int8       row 2560 B — still not a
        power of two, so it does not avoid (b); and it is a fidelity
        change, which this study has no measurement for.

    VERDICT ARITHMETIC: (a) costs 727.5 MiB of a channel that ends up 79 % full at W8;
    (b) costs new logic in a zero-margin timing path.  (a) is the
    cheap one unless the channel budget tightens.

